--TEST--
one string is asked of all five `Core\Net` doors and no door reads a scheme or a separator out of it to pick a transport — `rule:security/a-path-is-not-a-url`, `rule:core-classes/net-one-api-three-transports`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `unix:` is not a selector here and neither is the slash after it. Which
// transport a door opens is decided by which door it is, so the five members
// below refuse one string five different ways — each for the reason its own
// argument type gives, and none of them by inspecting the text for a prefix.
// A registry of schemes is what turns one file-read bug into a remote one, and
// there is nothing here for such a registry to hook into.
var $written = "unix:/run/novis/app.sock";

// Takes a host. A separator is the whole test: no hostname and no address
// literal carries one, so this is refused in front of the resolution rather
// than resolved and then denied.
try {
    Core\Net::connect($written, 80, 1s);
    echo "connected", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// Takes an endpoint, which is parsed and never resolved: a grant names one
// endpoint, and this string is not one.
try {
    Core\Net::listen($written, 0);
    echo "bound", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The third transport agrees with the second, because a bind is a bind
// whatever is bound at it.
try {
    Core\Net::bindDatagram($written, 0);
    echo "bound a datagram socket", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The two that do take a path take it as a path — scheme and all. Read as a
// path, the whole string does not start at a root, so each throws the
// relative-path error naming the whole string, before any grant is asked and
// without naming a transport.
try {
    Core\Net::connectLocal($written, 1s);
    echo "connected locally", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Core\Net::listenLocal($written);
    echo "bound locally", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# Both network grants, so the first three doors are refused by what they read
# rather than by what they are missing.
[capabilities.net]
connect = true
listen = true
--EXPECT--
Core\Net::connect refuses `unix:/run/novis/app.sock`: a socket path is not something this door can authorize, since the address policy `net.connect` carries has no address to read — the grant that answers a socket path is `net.local`, and it is asked by the member that takes a path rather than by this one, which takes a host
Core\Net::listen: `unix:/run/novis/app.sock` is not an IP address. A bind needs an endpoint, and a hostname is not one
Core\Net::bindDatagram: `unix:/run/novis/app.sock` is not an IP address. A bind needs an endpoint, and a hostname is not one
Core\Net::connectLocal needs an absolute path, and `unix:/run/novis/app.sock` is relative
help: build the path with `Core\Path::join` from a folder you know, or with `Core\Path::fromCwd` for a path typed on the command line
Core\Net::listenLocal needs an absolute path, and `unix:/run/novis/app.sock` is relative
help: build the path with `Core\Path::join` from a folder you know, or with `Core\Path::fromCwd` for a path typed on the command line
